OpaqueSheep OS Manual (Professional Shell OS)
============================================

This manual provides the in-depth APL (application) coding documentation for
OpaqueSheep OS. It is meant to be included with the OS and used as the primary
reference for writing high-level kernel logic and shell workflows.

Table of Contents
-----------------
1. Philosophy and Execution Model
2. Boot Flow and Startup System
3. APL Coding Model (High-Level API)
4. Shell Loop Patterns
5. File and Directory API
6. Error Handling and panic()
7. Extending Libraries
8. Vendor Configuration
9. Build and Run Reference

1) Philosophy and Execution Model
---------------------------------
OpaqueSheep OS is designed so that:
- `kmain()` reads like a script.
- All complex work lives in `kernel/libs/`.
- Each library is a single file for clarity and portability.

2) Boot Flow and Startup System
-------------------------------
- `kstup()` runs once at boot to initialize the OS.
- `kstup()` should call:
  - `stup_init()` (startup system)
  - `fat_init()` (filesystem scaffolding)
- `kmain()` is your main loop (shell, services, etc.).

3) APL Coding Model (High-Level API)
------------------------------------
Include the main header:

    #include "sys.h"

Available high-level calls:

- print(const char *message)
  Prints text to the console.

- input(const char *prompt)
  Prints the prompt (if provided) and returns the latest input line.

- fwrite(const void *data, size_t size, size_t count)
- fappend(const void *data, size_t size, size_t count)
- fread(void *data, size_t size, size_t count)
- fdelete(const char *path)

- dmake(const char *path)
- dremove(const char *path)
- dmove(const char *from, const char *to)

- fFormat(const char *label)
  Formats the default filesystem with an optional label.

- memcpy_simple(...), memset_simple(...)

- panic(const char *message)
  Halts the CPU in a fatal error state.

4) Shell Loop Patterns
----------------------
Minimal professional shell:

    void kmain(void) {
        print("OpaqueSheep Shell\n");
        print("Type 'help' for commands.\n");

        for (;;) {
            const char *cmd = input("> ");
            if (!cmd) {
                continue;
            }

            if (cmd[0] == 'h') {
                print("help - list commands\n");
            } else if (cmd[0] == 'f') {
                print("format - initialize filesystem\n");
            } else {
                print("unknown command\n");
            }
        }
    }

5) File and Directory API
-------------------------
Typical usage pattern:

    const char *data = "Hello\n";
    fwrite(data, 1, 6);
    fappend(data, 1, 6);

    dmake("/apps");
    dmove("/apps", "/system/apps");
    dremove("/system/apps");

    fFormat("OPAQUESHEEP");

6) Error Handling and panic()
-----------------------------
Use `panic()` only for fatal errors that cannot be recovered.

    if (!cmd) {
        panic("input() failed\n");
    }

7) Extending Libraries
----------------------
- Add new functions in `kernel/libs/<name>.c` and `<name>.h`.
- Expose them in `kernel/include/sys.h` if you want them available to `kmain()`.
- Keep the interface clean and high-level.

8) Vendor Configuration
-----------------------
`vendor.cfg` stores OS metadata for branding, versioning, and flavor selection.

Example:

    name = OpaqueSheep OS
    version = 1.1.1
    flavor = developer
    arch = x86_64
    boot = uefi

9) Build and Run Reference
--------------------------
Build:

    make
    make uefi

Run in QEMU (UEFI):

    qemu-system-x86_64 \
      -drive if=pflash,format=raw,readonly=on,file=/usr/share/OVMF/OVMF_CODE.fd \
      -drive if=pflash,format=raw,file=/usr/share/OVMF/OVMF_VARS.fd \
      -drive file=build/fat.img,format=raw
